rules_dotnet: honor `winexe` on binaries and tests

`csharp_binary`, `csharp_test`, `fsharp_binary` and `fsharp_test` declare a
`winexe` attribute ("If true, output a winexe-style executable, otherwise
output a console-style executable") and then compile with a hard-coded
`/target:exe`. The attribute has no effect: a program built for the Windows
subsystem (MSBuild `OutputType=WinExe`) comes out as a console program and
opens a console window when started.

Pass `winexe` through as the compiler's target kind. Nothing else in the rules
looks at the target kind of an executable.

Made against rules_dotnet 0.22.2. Apply with -p1.

diff --git a/dotnet/private/rules/csharp/binary.bzl b/dotnet/private/rules/csharp/binary.bzl
index 97c12ae..c074bf2 100644
--- a/dotnet/private/rules/csharp/binary.bzl
+++ b/dotnet/private/rules/csharp/binary.bzl
@@ -36,7 +36,7 @@ def _compile_action(ctx, tfm):
         appsetting_files = ctx.files.appsetting_files,
         compile_data = ctx.files.compile_data,
         out = ctx.attr.out,
-        target = "exe",
+        target = "winexe" if ctx.attr.winexe else "exe",
         target_name = ctx.attr.name,
         target_framework = tfm,
         toolchain = toolchain,
